diagnosis_text:
I verified inter-domain reachability end-to-end and the “translator” boundary is operating normally. From the user endpoints, pc0 (20.1.1.10) can ping pc12 (120.1.1.10) and vice versa with 0% loss and ~1.3–1.4 ms RTT, proving that the 20.x and 120.x sides are exchanging traffic successfully. On the 20.x side, pc0 has a default route via 20.1.1.1 and on the 120.x side, pc12 has a default via 120.1.1.1; both hosts are properly configured.

At the routing boundary, r11 (old side) is learning numerous prefixes via OSPF on B (toward r1) and EIGRP routes for the 102.x/103.x/113.x control/translation networks via 112.1.1.2 on eth0 (EE). r12 (new side) has 120.0.0.0/8 directly connected on eth1 (GG), a default route via 112.1.1.1 on eth0 (EE), and EIGRP-learned routes toward the translator and opposite side. These tables confirm the inter-domain exchange and next-hop adjacency are up across EE between r11 and r12.

Mechanism: successful bidirectional ICMP and consistent dynamic routing on the boundary indicate no isolation or filtering between 20.x and 120.x. Localization: r11–r12 boundary (EE) with EIGRP/OSPF redistribution is healthy. Root cause: no fault found; the network is currently married and functioning as intended. Corrective fix: none required. I recommend closing ticket #9139 and, if concerns persist, enabling short-term monitoring of EIGRP adjacency stability on EE during maintenance windows. 
